    },
    "method": "3x3-pixel median at 300 m vs laboratory chlorophyll-a (replicates at one pixel and day merged); log10 errors; 95% intervals by bootstrap over lake-days. Satellite = archived OLCI retrieval on the sample day (or +/-1 day). Forecast = issued L days before the sample from the archive; persistence = the newest satellite value in the forecast's input window; climatology = +/-15-day seasonal mean from other years.",
    "caveats": [
      "Field samples are point measurements, often near shore or at depth; a 300 m pixel averages the surface. Some disagreement is representativeness, not error.",
      "On training lakes the satellite inputs were seen during training (the field samples were not); held-out lakes are reported separately.",
      "Most samples are from US lakes (EPA WQP) and from 2016-2022 (GLORIA ends 2022)."
    ],
    "runtime_s": 407.2
  },
  "headline": [
    "On 371 same-day matchups across 11 lakes, the satellite ranked waters well (rank correlation 0.70) but read about 1.8x lower than the laboratory value on average; it was within a factor of 2 47% of the time.",
    "The 1-day forecast was closer to the field samples than repeating the last satellite image, but further from them than the lake's usual value for the time of year.",
    "Because of that low reading, the forecast flagged only 8% of field samples above 20 ug/L. A single correction learned from the other lakes raises that to 53%, at the cost of more false alarms \u2014 not yet applied."
  ],
